=== WHAT THIS PROVES (the Wave-6 GPU-aware kernel-cache milestone) ===
modal/app.py reuses the Todo 26 C++ Runtime DESIGN (lib/Runtime/: DeviceDetect ->
KernelCache -> Runtime) as a faithful Python selection layer:
  - detect: ModalGpuDetector maps the @app.cls gpu= class to its sm_ arch (the same
    string the C++ SmArchFromComputeCapability yields from cudaGetDeviceProperties;
    DeviceDetect.cpp). Declarative + GPU-free, so it runs in the snap=True pre-snapshot
    hook (no GPU yet). FixedArchDetector is the GPU-free mock (mirrors C++ FixedArchDetector).
  - select: KernelCache.select(gpu, op, shape) maps (arch, op, shape) -> the best VALIDATED
    contract-H cached variant, partitioned by gpu (mirrors C++ KernelCache::Select; gates on
    validated; graceful miss with BYTE-IDENTICAL error strings).
  - load + serve: the engine .so (dlopen'd in snap=True) IS the loaded compiled variant;
    /predict serves it for the selected config on a hit, and reports the graceful
    "no tuned kernel ...; run the autotuner" (HTTP 404) on a miss - never a wrong kernel,
    never a crash (mirror of C++ Runtime::Resolve).
Why a Python port (not ctypes to the C++ Runtime): the C++ PolyKernelRuntime lib is gated
behind hipcc/ROCm (lib/Runtime/CMakeLists.txt) but the Modal image is CUDA (nvidia/cuda, no
hipcc) and its LLVM toolchain is a documented deploy-time follow-up (modal/image.py) - so the
C++ Runtime cannot be baked into the Modal image today. The tuning DB + compiled variants ARE
baked into the image (TUNING_CACHE_PATH, the same contract-H JSON /kernels reads) or on a Volume.

